WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.502
- It tends to be White people,

00:00:01.502 --> 00:00:03.449
when they're just trying to
figure out what to call you.

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:08.026
They're just like "she's
Bla (mumbles) and African,

00:00:08.026 --> 00:00:11.532
"I don't know, (laughs), she's my friend!"

00:00:15.066 --> 00:00:17.270
- I grew up identifying as Black.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.290
- I identify as a Black man.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.989
- I think I identify, I call myself Black,

00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:24.400
just because I'm still
trying to trace my ancestry.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.404
- I identify as African American.

00:00:26.404 --> 00:00:28.632
- I identify as Black,
but I'm also Creole.

00:00:28.632 --> 00:00:32.824
- I am Bayesian American
and Nigerian American,

00:00:32.824 --> 00:00:35.195
but I, honestly, I call myself Black.

00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:37.898
- Black is beautiful,

00:00:37.898 --> 00:00:40.101
and I've always just found
strength in the word.

00:00:40.101 --> 00:00:41.985
Black is strong, Black is pretty.

00:00:41.985 --> 00:00:44.142
- Depending on the situation
or the moment in time,

00:00:44.142 --> 00:00:45.330
I feel hella proud,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.534
like incredibly proud to be a Black man,

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:50.540
and Black Power, all
that other good stuff.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:51.217
But then--

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.376
- To society, there's a lot of negative.

00:00:53.376 --> 00:00:55.092
So Black is synonymous with ghetto,

00:00:55.092 --> 00:00:57.924
it's synonymous with loud,
it's synonymous with lazy.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:59.620
- The only time that it really bothers me

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.652
is when it's used amongst
people who look like me.

00:01:02.652 --> 00:01:03.848
Someone from the Caribbean,

00:01:03.848 --> 00:01:06.370
or someone from a country
in Africa, that are saying--

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.766
- I'm not Black, I'm Nigerian,

00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:09.885
or I'm not Black, I'm Bayesian.

00:01:09.885 --> 00:01:10.886
- Members of my family,

00:01:10.886 --> 00:01:13.510
they wanna separate themselves
from Black Americans,

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.737
because they think that
being too tied to that

00:01:16.737 --> 00:01:17.849
means that they're ghetto.

00:01:17.849 --> 00:01:20.307
- We should be on a much
stronger unified front.

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:23.052
- It's interesting for me,

00:01:23.052 --> 00:01:25.477
because I technically am African American.

00:01:25.477 --> 00:01:27.058
- I mean me personally I feel like,

00:01:27.058 --> 00:01:29.774
African American is a label
that was created for us.

00:01:29.774 --> 00:01:31.305
- It just seems like
something that would be

00:01:31.305 --> 00:01:33.804
in like the New York Times
to describe Black people.

00:01:33.804 --> 00:01:35.270
- I'm not from Africa directly,

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.151
so I don't feel like I should label myself

00:01:37.151 --> 00:01:38.498
as an African American.

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:40.730
- I think for some people
it just feels wrong

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.513
to hearken back to
something that they now,

00:01:43.513 --> 00:01:45.837
unfortunately don't really
have a connection to.

00:01:45.837 --> 00:01:48.645
- I sometimes refer to
myself as Ghanaian American,

00:01:48.645 --> 00:01:50.843
just because, I mean that's what I am.

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:55.357
African American is still
such a wide and broad term.

00:01:55.357 --> 00:01:56.353
- My family is from Haiti,

00:01:56.353 --> 00:01:59.514
so I never really tied
myself to African American.

00:01:59.514 --> 00:02:02.505
- There's 90 something
countries in Africa, right?

00:02:02.505 --> 00:02:04.273
- Just like there are varying
types of White people,

00:02:04.273 --> 00:02:05.689
there are varying types of Black people.

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:08.103
- I don't think there's
anything wrong with that term.

00:02:08.103 --> 00:02:10.913
I think it just depends on where you are,

00:02:10.913 --> 00:02:12.984
and finding yourself and your ancestry.

00:02:14.724 --> 00:02:17.976
It's just kind of
interesting how as a people,

00:02:17.976 --> 00:02:19.730
we find identity with certain words.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:20.752
- And maybe that's the other thing,

00:02:20.752 --> 00:02:23.284
is that how you identify
with it is just fluid.

00:02:23.284 --> 00:02:24.676
- I think bein' Black is dope.

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:26.416
I think if you are rejecting it,

00:02:26.416 --> 00:02:28.507
you're missing out on a lot of fun shit.

00:02:28.507 --> 00:02:30.827
- Regardless of your
race and your ethnicity,

00:02:30.827 --> 00:02:32.199
your background so to speak,

00:02:32.199 --> 00:02:33.665
just be proud of who you are.

00:02:33.665 --> 00:02:38.096
("The Gang")

